  },
  "locales": {
    "en": {
      "title": "Impersonal constructions",
      "summary": "An impersonal construction has a dummy subject such as it or there.",
      "status": "draft",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "What it is",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "An impersonal construction has no real subject. English uses it, there or you."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "**It** is raining.",
                "**It** is said that he is rich.",
                "**There** is no time."
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "How it works in English",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "It is used for weather, time and judgements, and there for existence. A common pattern is it, be, an adjective and to: It is important to rest."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "html": "<table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>weather</td><td><strong>It is snowing.</strong></td></tr><tr><td>time</td><td><strong>It is late.</strong></td></tr><tr><td>judgement</td><td><strong>It is easy to forget.</strong></td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
